const weekOneSlides = {"unit":1,"week":1,"licence":"CC BY 4.0","codeLicence":"MIT","chapter":"start","source":"https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#start","references":["https://docs.python.org/3/library/functions.html#input","https://docs.python.org/3/library/functions.html#int","https://docs.python.org/3/library/functions.html#print"],"lessons":[{"id":"u1-w01-l01","lesson":1,"title":"Plan a badge-cost program","prepare":"Whiteboard or paper; chapter open. No Python installation needed for this lesson.","slides":[{"title":"Plan a badge-cost program","body":["Unit 1 · Week 1 · Lesson 1","Explain the problem before writing code."],"minutes":2,"notes":"Introduce the week: plan, run, trace/test, then apply. Ask learners to explain what a successful badge calculator would do. This lesson is diagnostic and formative.","strategy":"Learning intention"},{"title":"What does a program need?","body":["A club sells badges for $3 each.","Think alone: what input do we need?","Compare with a partner: what should four badges cost?","Share your method, then try six badges."],"minutes":5,"notes":"Allow 45 seconds of silent thinking, one minute comparing, then invite two explanations. Expected: quantity is input, 4 × 3 = $12, 6 × 3 = $18. Accept diagrams or spoken explanations. If learners add 3 to the quantity, use equal groups to model the price per badge.","strategy":"Think, pair, share"},{"title":"Today’s goal","body":["I can identify input, processing and output.","I can write calculation steps in order.","I can predict a result before testing."],"minutes":6,"notes":"Have learners restate one criterion in their own words. Distinguish the variable quantity from the stated fixed price. Return to these criteria at the exit check.","strategy":"Success criteria"},{"title":"The badge requirement","body":["Display the cost for a valid badge quantity.","Each badge costs $3.","Assume a whole-number quantity of zero or more.","Input validation is taught later."],"minutes":10,"notes":"Think aloud as you underline quantity, circle the price and identify the required output. Model why zero is allowed by the stated requirement. Ask everyone to identify one valid input before continuing. If several learners choose a negative quantity, revisit the assumption. Validation comes later.","strategy":"Teacher modelling"},{"title":"A worked example: four badges","table":[["Stage","Badge example"],["Input","Quantity: 4"],["Processing","4 × 3 = 12"],["Output","Total: $12"]],"minutes":10,"notes":"Model input 4, processing 4 × 3 and output Total: $12, explaining each decision. Then learners create the same three rows for quantity 6 without copying the numbers. Ask everyone to show their processing row. Expected $18. If they write 6 + 3, model equal groups again before independent practice.","strategy":"Worked example and guided practice"},{"title":"An algorithm for any quantity","body":["Write the steps without looking at the example.","Trace your steps for 0, 1 and 6 badges.","Explain your sequence to a partner.","Revise any step your partner cannot follow."],"minutes":12,"notes":"Allow six minutes independently, three minutes of explanation in pairs, then three minutes to revise. Expected sequence: read quantity, multiply by 3, display total. Expected totals $0, $3, $18. Support: offer the three stage names Input, Processing, Output. Stretch: explain why displaying total before assigning it fails. Keep individual plans as evidence.","strategy":"Independent practice and peer explanation"},{"title":"Which rule matches the requirement?","body":["For six badges, choose a rule on paper.","A: quantity + 3     B: quantity × 3","Show your choice together.","Explain what the other rule gets wrong."],"minutes":5,"notes":"Collect a simultaneous response before discussion so every learner commits. Correct B gives $18. A gives $9 and adds a price once rather than charging for each badge. If several choose A, use a drawing of six groups of $3 and ask a fresh check for two badges before moving on.","strategy":"All-student misconception check"},{"title":"Exit check: seven badges","body":["Name the input.","State the processing step.","Give the expected displayed total.","Explain why an expected result is useful."],"minutes":5,"notes":"Collect an individual written response without partner help. Expected input quantity 7, processing multiply by 3, Total: $21. An independent expectation checks correctness. Sort responses into ready, input/processing confusion and calculation support, and use these groups to plan the next lesson starter.","strategy":"Independent exit ticket"}]},{"id":"u1-w01-l02","lesson":2,"title":"Run your first Python script","prepare":"School-approved Python 3 editor/IDE, writable practice folder and projector. Check the school’s Run command before class.","slides":[{"title":"Run your first Python script","body":["Unit 1 · Week 1 · Lesson 2","Turn the badge algorithm into a saved program."],"minutes":2,"notes":"Use the school-approved environment. Do not ask learners to install software or change school settings during the lesson.","strategy":"Learning intention"},{"title":"Retrieve the badge plan","body":["Close your notes for one minute.","Name the input and calculation step.","Predict the cost of six badges.","Compare with your notes and correct one detail."],"minutes":5,"notes":"Use one minute of individual retrieval, then compare and correct. Expected quantity, quantity × 3, $18. Ask everyone to show the calculation. If confusion remains between input and processing, briefly return to the three-row badge example before opening the editor.","strategy":"Retrieval practice"},{"title":"Today’s goal","body":["Save and run a Python script.","Enter suitable data when prompted.","Compare actual output with a prediction."],"minutes":6,"notes":"Success: the learner saves badge_cost.py, runs it independently, identifies where to enter the quantity and records a matching prediction/result.","strategy":"Success criteria"},{"title":"From demonstration to your own file","body":["Watch the teacher save badge_cost.py.","Point out the editor and output area.","Create your own file in this week’s folder.","Show your partner where Save and Run are."],"minutes":10,"notes":"Demonstrate in the school-approved editor for three minutes, then pause for learners to repeat each step. Ask partners to point to controls on their own screens, without taking over. Check everyone has a saved .py file before continuing. Save stores changes and Run executes them. Provide a prepared folder or printed instructions for setup support. Avoid filenames that shadow modules, such as random.py.","strategy":"Demonstration with guided rehearsal"},{"title":"The badge script","code":"BADGE_PRICE = 3\nquantity = int(input(\"Number of badges: \"))\ntotal = quantity * BADGE_PRICE\nprint(f\"Total: ${total}\")","minutes":10,"notes":"Read the code top to bottom and connect each line to the paper plan. Ask learners to point to the line that reads input before you explain it. input returns text and int converts suitable whole-number text. Pause before the total line and elicit a prediction for quantity 4. print displays the result. Uppercase BADGE_PRICE signals an intended fixed value, but Python does not enforce it. Model the reasoning rather than only typing.","strategy":"Code reading and teacher think-aloud"},{"title":"A prediction before each run","body":["Write your prediction for four badges.","Run the saved script and enter 4.","Compare expected and actual output with a partner.","Repeat for 1 and 0, predicting each time."],"minutes":12,"notes":"Use individual predictions before learners work in pairs. Driver runs the file and navigator checks the prediction and explains a line, then swap for the next quantity. Expected $12, $3, $0. If a result differs, inspect the saved file and input before changing code. Each learner retains a prediction/result record. Use suitable inputs only.","strategy":"Predict, run, investigate"},{"title":"One change, a new prediction","body":["Change BADGE_PRICE from 3 to 5.","Predict four badges before running.","Explain why the input line stays the same.","Return the price to 3 after checking."],"minutes":5,"notes":"Expected $20. Ask all learners to write why quantity still needs input while the fixed price changes. If the output stays $12, check saving and which file is running. Support: highlight the price line. Stretch: predict a new price and quantity before testing. Keep the original file structure.","strategy":"Controlled variation and explanation"},{"title":"Exit check: run independently","body":["Show your saved Python file.","Run it for two badges at $3 each.","State the expected output before running.","Explain the difference between Save and Run."],"minutes":5,"notes":"Each learner saves and runs their own file for quantity 2 at price 3, predicting Total: $6 first. Ask for a spoken or written explanation of Save versus Run. Record setup difficulties separately from conceptual errors. Start the next lesson with a short demonstration for learners who need environment support.","strategy":"Independent performance check"}]},{"id":"u1-w01-l03","lesson":3,"title":"Trace, modify and test a sequence","prepare":"Working badge_cost.py at price 3; paper trace table or worksheet; Python editor.","slides":[{"title":"Trace, modify and test","body":["Unit 1 · Week 1 · Lesson 3","Explain what changes as each instruction runs."],"minutes":2,"notes":"Learners should already be able to run the badge script. Keep a clean copy available so setup errors do not consume the tracing lesson.","strategy":"Learning intention"},{"title":"Retrieve the script","code":"BADGE_PRICE = 3\nquantity = int(input(\"Number of badges: \"))\ntotal = quantity * BADGE_PRICE\nprint(f\"Total: ${total}\")","body":["Predict the output for quantity 5."],"minutes":5,"notes":"Learners predict Total: $15 individually before running. Ask them to annotate which line reads input, which calculates and which displays. Compare annotations in pairs. If they treat print as the calculation, trace the total assignment explicitly before moving on.","strategy":"Retrieval and code annotation"},{"title":"Today’s goal","body":["Trace variable values in execution order.","Modify a calculation to match a requirement.","Record expected and actual results."],"minutes":6,"notes":"Success: complete a trace for a new input, add a fixed charge correctly, and explain at least one meaningful test. A trace records state after each instruction, not just the final answer.","strategy":"Success criteria"},{"title":"Trace four badges","table":[["After instruction","Value known"],["BADGE_PRICE = 3","BADGE_PRICE is 3"],["Read quantity 4","quantity is 4"],["Calculate total","total is 12"],["Display total","Output: Total: $12"]],"minutes":10,"notes":"Think aloud through the model for quantity 4. Cover the model or move off this slide and ask learners to trace quantity 2 using blank rows. Expected price 3, quantity 2, total 6 and output Total: $6. total has no assigned value before its calculation. Support: retain instruction labels. Stretch: explain which values remain unchanged at each line. Check every learner’s total row before the next task.","strategy":"Worked trace and faded support"},{"title":"Add a fixed packaging charge","code":"PACKAGING = 2\ntotal = quantity * BADGE_PRICE + PACKAGING","body":["The $2 charge applies to every order, including zero badges.","Predict the totals for quantities 0 and 3."],"minutes":10,"notes":"Model adding PACKAGING = 2 above the total calculation. Learners explain in pairs why packaging is added once, then independently predict quantity 0 ($2) and 3 ($11). Check answers before implementation. Contrast the stated zero-order rule with possible alternative requirements without changing this task. Multiplication precedes addition here.","strategy":"Guided prediction and code modification"},{"title":"Test the modified program","table":[["Quantity","Expected","Actual"],["0","$2","Record your run"],["1","$5","Record your run"],["3","$11","Record your run"]],"minutes":12,"notes":"Driver implements and runs the modification. Navigator checks the requirement and compares expected with actual output. Swap roles after the first case. Every learner keeps their own test record. Expected $2, $5, $11. Keep expected values unchanged when actual differs. Support: provide the correct input and output lines. Stretch: find a quantity where an incorrect packaging-per-item calculation accidentally passes (quantity 1).","strategy":"Paired testing with role changes"},{"title":"Two rules, different costs","body":["For quantity 3, which rule charges packaging once?","A: quantity × 3 + 2     B: quantity × (3 + 2)","Choose alone, then explain to a partner.","Why does a test with quantity 1 miss the error?"],"minutes":5,"notes":"Take simultaneous A/B responses before partner discussion. A gives $11 and B gives $15. Both are syntactically valid, so B is a logic error for this requirement. Quantity 1 produces $5 for both and cannot distinguish them. If several choose B, model one packaging charge separately and recheck quantity 2: $8 versus $10. A syntax error prevents Python parsing the instructions.","strategy":"Hinge question and error analysis"},{"title":"Exit check: a changed order","body":["Badges cost $3 each. Packaging is $2 per order.","Predict the cost of six badges.","Give the expected result for quantity 0.","Explain which test could reveal a packaging error."],"minutes":5,"notes":"Collect individual responses: quantity 6 gives $20 and quantity 0 gives $2. A zero test exposes the packaging-per-item fault because it gives $0. Look for a requirement-based explanation and an independent expected result. Use errors to choose a brief trace or fixed-charge starter next lesson.","strategy":"Independent exit ticket"}]},{"id":"u1-w01-l04","lesson":4,"title":"Build a book-cover calculator","prepare":"Python editor, a new cover_cost.py file and a test-record template. Keep the badge example available as a reference.","slides":[{"title":"Build a book-cover calculator","body":["Unit 1 · Week 1 · Lesson 4","Plan, implement, test and explain a new problem."],"minutes":2,"notes":"This is formative transfer practice, not an additional summative assessment. Let learners use their earlier notes while making their own design and test decisions.","strategy":"Learning intention and transfer"},{"title":"Retrieve a useful test habit","body":["Write why we predict before running.","Explain a fixed charge using the badge example.","Compare explanations with a partner.","Add one detail that makes your answer clearer."],"minutes":5,"notes":"Allow one minute of writing, two minutes comparing, then two minutes sharing corrections. Expected: an independent prediction checks correctness, a fixed charge applies once per order and a trace finds the first wrong value. Invite learners to use a small numerical example if language is a barrier.","strategy":"Retrieval with peer explanation"},{"title":"The book-cover requirement","body":["Each book cover costs $2.","Every order has a $4 handling charge.","The handling charge also applies to a zero-cover order.","Assume the quantity is a whole number of zero or more."],"minutes":6,"notes":"Learners underline the input, circle the two prices and mark the zero-order rule in their own notes. Ask everyone to predict whether quantity 0 costs $0 or $4 and explain. Correct $4 under this requirement. Success means an IPO plan, readable saved script, three predicted tests and an explanation of a line. Validation comes later.","strategy":"Requirement annotation"},{"title":"Design before coding","body":["Draft your own input, processing and output plan.","Write the calculation in words.","Ask a partner to follow the steps for two covers.","Revise your plan before coding."],"minutes":10,"notes":"Five minutes independent design, three minutes partner walkthrough, two minutes revision. Expected quantity × 2 + 4, so two covers cost $8. Partners ask questions without writing the solution for each other. Support: offer a blank IPO frame. Stretch: explain how a change to handling would affect every order. Suggested names COVER_PRICE and HANDLING.","strategy":"Independent design and peer critique"},{"title":"Expected results come from the rule","table":[["Covers","Your working","Expected total"],["0","Calculate on paper","Record prediction"],["1","Calculate on paper","Record prediction"],["5","Calculate on paper","Record prediction"]],"minutes":10,"notes":"Learners calculate all three cases individually before any program runs. Expected 0 × 2 + 4 = $4, 1 × 2 + 4 = $6 and 5 × 2 + 4 = $14. Ask for working, then compare in pairs. Keep answers in teacher notes so the slide does not give away the task. Support: model a different quantity, 2 gives $8. Stretch: find a test that distinguishes adding handling once from adding it per cover.","strategy":"Independent prediction with reduced support"},{"title":"Your own working calculator","body":["Create and save cover_cost.py.","Use your plan to write the calculation.","Display a labelled result and run your tests.","Record actual results beside your predictions."],"minutes":12,"notes":"Allow independent work, with short teacher conferences. Offer the badge example or a partial file only to learners who need it, then ask them to explain the changed lines. Reference: COVER_PRICE = 2; HANDLING = 4; quantity = int(input('Covers: ')); total = quantity * COVER_PRICE + HANDLING; print(f'Total: ${total}'). Stretch: change one fixed value, design a fresh test and justify it. Do not introduce input validation in this task.","strategy":"Independent practice with optional scaffolds"},{"title":"Feedback that leads to a revision","body":["Show a partner one prediction and actual result.","Explain why handling is added once.","Partner: ask one question about the evidence.","Revise one detail and name a limitation."],"minutes":5,"notes":"Each learner explains while the partner checks against the requirement. Expected test results $4, $6, $14. Feedback should identify a specific match or mismatch, not just say good work. Learners revise their own code or explanation. A suitable limitation is that invalid text and negative quantities are not handled. Keep the original expectation if a run fails.","strategy":"Peer feedback and self-explanation"},{"title":"Exit check: a fresh quantity","body":["Predict three covers without running the code.","Explain one input, processing or output line.","Which check helped you find or avoid an error?","Save your script and test evidence."],"minutes":5,"notes":"Collect individual responses before showing the answer: $10. Ask for a specific check such as predicting zero, tracing a total or comparing an actual result. Use responses to identify learners needing setup, arithmetic or tracing support before next week’s types and operators. The task remains formative.","strategy":"Independent exit ticket and reflection"}]}]};
